{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high vs. realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially in downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Trend/lag error in selloffs: when recent history is rolling over, the model underestimates the speed and depth of declines (forecasts flatten or rise while outcomes keep falling). Increase downside momentum sensitivity (steepen negative slope).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following late-history spikes/high levels (e.g., low 80s), the model projects continued strength or only mild pullback, but outcomes mean-revert sharply lower. Add stronger mean-reversion after elevated regimes.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors widen with forecast horizon (later days are most overestimated). Use a horizon-increasing negative correction (e.g., larger downward adjustment after ~day 10–15).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: model path is too smooth and centered high; realized series shows larger drawdowns. Increase downside tail/vol allowance and reduce upward drift in stressed regimes."
  ]
}
